{
  "id": "r-07",
  "level": 2,
  "title": "select: picking columns",
  "explanation": "Real tables have lots of columns you don't need. **select()** keeps only the columns you name, in the order you name them. This lesson uses `players`, a table of gamers with their country, signup date and hours played.",
  "datasets": [
    "players"
  ],
  "starter_code": "",
  "tasks": [
    {
      "teach": "Inside dplyr functions, you write column names **without quotes** and **without** `players$`. dplyr knows they're columns of the piped table.",
      "prompt": "Keep just the username and hours_played columns, and store the result in result: result <- players |> select(username, hours_played).",
      "starter_code": "",
      "check": {
        "type": "data_frame",
        "object": "result",
        "columns": [
          "username",
          "hours_played"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "ShadowFox",
            320
          ],
          [
            "pixelqueen",
            145
          ],
          [
            "NoScopeNate",
            610
          ],
          [
            "luna_plays",
            88
          ],
          [
            "BlockBuilder",
            275
          ],
          [
            "speedy_g",
            40
          ],
          [
            "CaptainCrit",
            198
          ],
          [
            "mochi",
            12
          ],
          [
            "TurboTaco",
            133
          ],
          [
            "xXDragonXx",
            502
          ]
        ],
        "ignore_order": false,
        "must_use": [
          "select"
        ]
      },
      "hints": [
        "Column names go inside select(), separated by commas. No quotes needed.",
        "result <- players |> select(username, hours_played)"
      ],
      "answer": "result <- players |> select(username, hours_played)"
    },
